Hannah’s hips ached as she sat up, the price she knew she'd pay in the morning now here. She fumbles in her bedside drawer for a couple of ibuprofen tablets, slightly embarrassed at age forty-one,  all her body needed now to go out of commission was a night of great sex. Eventually finding the drive to get herself out of bed when it was clear Laura wasn’t returning anytime soon. Hannah pulls on her dressing gown, found abandoned at the side of the bed where Laura had discarded it. She could smell her on the fabric, Hannah realised as she walked slowly down the stairs, feeling old beyond her years as she clung to the bannister.
‘Morning,’ Laura smiles, her voice still gravelly from sleep. She looked so natural, standing in Hannah’s kitchen, her hair damp and messy, just making breakfast. Hannah felt slightly ashamed that she was wearing Jonas’s robe, though. She’d need to remember to put that in the wash along with the sheets before he got home. It suited her, though.
‘You’re up early,’ Hannah says gently, making herself a mug of tea. Laura was slightly pleased that her hatred of his coffee had been proven true, if only as it made her feel slightly less irrational.
‘Force of habit,’ Laura hums, nudging at the egg in the frying pan. ‘Couldn’t get back to sleep and I didn't want to wake you up… looked too cosy,’ a slight flush appeared in her cheeks. ‘You want breakfast?’ she asks.
‘Oh .. umm, sure,’ Hannah nods. ‘You need a hand? –’
‘It’s fried egg on toast, I can manage.’ Laura sighs, ‘Unless you think I'm going to scratch up your frying pan… or I don't know– set fire to your kitchen.’
‘I was just trying to help.’
‘Just let me make you breakfast, Hannah. I’m a big girl, I can manage.’
Hannah decides it wasn't worth the hassle, doing her best not to micromanage, even when she was sure Laura had forgotten the toast or used far too much oil on the pan. She instead tried to focus on enjoying that someone was making her breakfast, and it wasn't even mothers day or her birthday. She hadn't even had to ask.  Her worries had been baseless, of course, when she started eating.
‘Did I do alright?’ Laura asks, hand covering her mouth as she chews.
‘No complaints,’ Hannah says coyly, before taking another bite. "It's good, thanks.’ She likes seeing Laura smile, hating herself for having always used compliments as a tool to get what she wanted. She could have had this happy, relaxed version of Laura for far longer if Hannah had let herself. The familiar, funny feeling that this was another glimpse into a life she could have had filled her brain, making her chest tight. How many quiet mornings like this could she have had, how much she’d like her life if it had looked like this all the time. Hannah would have liked them even more if Laura were with her, so domestic sat quietly across from each other. Hannah wanted to wake up next to her every morning, even if Laura hadn't made her breakfast.
‘You alright in there?’ Laura clicks her fingers in front of Hannah’s face. ‘I know I can be dull sometimes–’
‘Sorry… zoned out for a minute…late night, I think.’ Hannah smiles, trying to push the ache inside her chest out of her mind and enjoy Laura’s company while she is here with her.
