The house felt distinctly empty after Laura had finally left. Hannah had suggested that she could stay for lunch, but Laura had her own life and responsibilities to attend to before she flew out to Austin on Tuesday; namely, finishing off the final bits of post-race analysis after Singapore that she’d been forced to abandon yesterday while shmoozing Haas’s sponsors. The practicalities did little to lessen the ache Hannah felt when she'd kissed her goodbye tenderly and left her all alone. Hannah felt such guilt that she didn’t really want her family back, at least, not so soon. Even if she was leaving again on Tuesday, she wanted to spend the rest of her Sunday relaxing, instead of running around the house making sure things were perfect (and there were no missed signs of Laura) around her home for their return. Hannah tells herself she’ll feel better once she’s seen the kids, even if things with their father were hard, she still treasured them. The constant bickering and demands on her time, less so.
The smell of sex still lingered in her bedroom. Opening a window, she lights the candle she knows Jonas hates, trying to remove the smell. She’d much rather have the argument about her being selfish and that it was giving him a headache, than to explain to him why their bedroom smelled like another woman.  She works quickly, stripping the bed, removing every possible trace that she hadn’t spent the night alone. The second-to-last pillow still smells like Laura, and Hannah doesn’t want to change it, deciding that she’ll keep that pillow on her side of the bed instead. Pathetically, she stills a moment, holding it close to her. This was only meant to be sex, Hannah thinks; she hadn’t meant to fall in love with her. The realisation hits her harder, feeling as if all the air had been knocked from her. She knew she was gay; both liberating and terrifying, as Hannah allows herself to finally acknowledge it. She also knew, more than anything, her marriage was over.
